this does not describe the purpose of escalation as defined and set yesterday.

**Escalation use: the only sanctioned researcher interaction. all runtime errors is reported in it; both Claude and Researcher record emerging issues, tasks, followups as feedback or to get feedback. It is the authorative record of open items in the project. It will pause a running process, and allow to resume at resume_point when answered.**

Comments on §2 The table

The table have most of the fields defined that would be required.  The current routine does not allow for proper updating of the fields, neither does not respond to the content of the fields appropriately.

The lack of a history record is the single most critical failure of this system.

The table design should continue to use escalation as the current status of the id.
I history table must be written for each update to the table.
escalation should include the version number for each id, to correspond to the history table.
the comment and context in escalation is not cummulative. the full story of the item is the sum total of all the history rows for the item. 

escalation table columns
ID = NNNN-NN : serial number (4 digits) + Version (2 digits) each item runs its own version increments - this comes from history record.
source = what triggered the item: script name|module|issue area
at_step= only used for pipeline reference if code generated
short_description = label or title of item.  What this item is about.
context = what must be done or error message, and link to external documents
Comment = additional information for the re-assign party
tried = record of the iterations of self correction
answered_at = history record datetime
raised_at = first creation datetime
resolution = what was done
related_activity = links this item with any other items that relates to it. Item id
next action assigned to = Claude / Researcher
answered_by : change this to originator - this it automatically updated from the history record creator.


comment on §3 state machine

The state machine is currently built around decision response. This must change. every step destination is not a decision.

raised = new open item
re-assign = mechanism to raise the attention of a party, show who has responsibility for the current action
on-hold = action is paused.
in-progress = item is worked on. multiple iterations of updates is likely
closed = item has no actions associated and need no further attention.
completed = item actions have all been fulfilled and validated.
withdraw = item is no longer required.

answered and paused is already covered above, then can be removed.

type = differentiate to type of item. active list is fine

next action = what is expected to be done. Note these are not decisions. the current list does not make sense.  I suggest

approve = the comment + context is accepted , re-assigned party can act on the full history context.
reject = the item is rejected. comment + context provide detail for the decision
revise = this item need to be revised based on the comment + context

hold is already a state, so does not need to be an action also

comments for §4 entry points
the entry point for errors must be adapted to put the right values in the right columns per the new definition 
the entry point for user interface (PS) is much more involved because it means you need to have checks for data entry completeness.
processing the data entry will change as it need to write to history
you will also need a second processor which will be triggered by the history update, to update the escalation.

comments for §5 entry points
most of the items in this section will need to change or be replaced.  your plan should have a similar table but working through the new logic for updating from history. 
it also need a second table to show the knock on effect on what you need to do to create a update / new record and what you should put into the different columns
the whole intent is that you would consume, based on different triggers and you would push either new or updates based on triggers.

following working through everything, I think it is feasible, and would be less work to re-write the escalation routine to make it fit for purpose, rather than implement a new application.

